Date: Fri, 1 Oct 93 18:09:28 PDT
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: BLACKNET:  The truth is thought provoking

For the record, I don't think it's nearly as easy to shut down
BlackNet-crypto activities as Charlie Stross thinks:

> Last night, at Tim's prompting, I tried to work out how easily they could shut 
> down Blacknet, should it evolve. The answer was, "very easily". It's trivial to
> construct a filter algorithm that can distinguish data transmissions from voice
> on the basis of entropy. Check data transmissions off-line to identify fax
> headers, and eliminate faxes; you're left with modems. Check phone numbers for
> "legitimate" corporate accounts and you're left with a relatively small number

I agreed last night with Charlie that entropy measurements and other
signal statistics could probably distinguish _voice_ transmissions
from _encrypted_ transmissions. This does not mean BlackNet sorts of
things can be "clamped down" on at all easily, for several reasons:

1. Distinguishing voice from data is easy today, with the right
equipment, but the real problem is, rather, distinguishing "bad"
("evil") data from "good" data...a much harder problem. 

2. Data transmissions are ubiquitous. Finding the encrypted traffic in
data is tough--a topic that comes up every time the proposals to ban
crypto get debated.

3. Take for example the mini-BlackNet example I ran recently on the
List. What would "entropy measures" or other statistical measures have
done? (Agreed, if the intent is to shut down all e-mail, all funds
transfers, all corporate communications....this is too large a blow
fro even the Feds to consider.)

So, my friend Charlie is right that DSP techniques can distinguish
between most data transmissions (high entropy) and most voice
transmissions (low entropy, unless compressed first, which will be
more common in the future) but he is wrong that this means a crackdown
on strong crypto is enforceable without massive disruption of all
digital communications.

> 
> -- Charlie (growing ever more paranoid ...)
> 
> 

And justifiably so...

The changes some of us are talking about will happen long before the
benefits of nanotechnology every appear. Dare I call it the "Crypto
Singularity"? 


-Tim May


