Subject: HEALTH: Burning your Health Card

> Eli, penicillin isn't a relatively benign drug. I'm probably allergic
> to it and usually end up having to take something else for infections.

That's why it's *relatively* benign... one of its problems is that
it has a high incidence of allergic reactions.  Topical penicillin
is definitely out.  Your allergy is not at all unusual -- some
people find out about it during IV penicillin administration.  But
penicillins are generally benign compared to, say, doxycycline.

> Of more relevance: antibiotics in particular are supposed to be mor
> oops, more carefully rationed than that in order to keep more resistant
> strains of the disease from appearing. 

Yup.  As I said, this is arguably the major downside of this sort
of treatment.

> This isn't really relevant to a viral infection, but I normally try
> not to go to the doctor when I have a simple cold.

Good idea.  First, the chance of picking up something nasty in the
waiting room probably outweighs anything you could gain.  Second,
once you show up with symptoms, they're probably going to have to
do something, for reasons of medico-legal ass-covering.  This may
not be ideal in a strictly medical sense.

Actually, selecting for resistant strains is a problem even with
a viral infection, or no infection.  People routinely harbor
pathogenic species in their normal bacterial flora.  I personally
see stronger externality arguments for controlling antibiotics than
for any other class of drugs I can think of.

