From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: BLACKNET:  The truth is thought provoking

Charlie's post raises a question  I have been concerned about for a while.
I have an article in draft on the Clipper Chip and the wider issues raised
by cryptography, and hope to publish it in a law review. It includes a
discussion of the downside of strong privacy--ways in which encryption
allows criminal firms to take advantage of brand name and economies of
scale to produce undesirable things, such as contract killings, much more
effectively than they now do.

My basic instinct is that, in arguing for a proposition (in this case
unregulated cryptography) one has an obligation to present and deal with
the strongest arguments against it. That is part of the reason I included
that section. On the other hand, I am a little worried about giving
ammunition to the other side. The public discussion of the disadvantages of
encryption so far seems to be limited to the argument that it interferes
with wire taps--which in my view is only the tip of the iceberg. If I
convince people that widespread cryptography has serious adverse
consequences, am I doing more harm than good? Or is the other side likely
to make these arguments in any case? Have they?

David Friedman

