Some May say:
> The part cited above about "make any thing but gold and silver coin a
> tender in payment of debts..." I take to be fully consistent with my
> point. That is, the Constitution defined money to be "coin" and
> forbade any state from doing an end-run around this requirement that
> debts be payable in gold and silver coin.

Note that everything in that paragraph of prohibitions to the States
is a power either explicitly assigned (treaties, letters of marque, coining
money) or explicitly denied (bills of attainder, ex post facto law, title
of nobility) to the United States, with these exceptions: bills of credit
[whatever that means], paper money, laws impairing contract.

Note also that at least one of the Federalist Papers says that a folly such
as "a passion for paper money, or a cancellation of debts" is less likely
to take hold in a large republic than in a small.  (Wish I had an e-version
of the FP to search.)  I think, though I can't say with confidence, that
Publius said paper money was a power held in reserve for emergencies like
war.

