In <18010@price.demon.co.uk>, price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)  wrote:

> These are compatible statements.  Well almost.  The dictionary said
> _definable_, not _defined_, which might explain James' problems with

The Random house Webster's college dictionary 1990 says:

The view that experimental operations *must define*
scientific terms and concepts.

(emphasis added)

(This definition is of course fundamentally incompatible
with science and the scientific method.)

The Webster's New collegiate dictionary, from which Price
appears to be quoting says "definable in terms of
*identifiable* and repeatable operations."

(emphasis added)

The Websters Collegiate definition is less obviously
incompatible with science and the scientific method, but is
still has the same disastrous defects as the Random House
definition, though to a lesser degree.

Since Price has been clearly unable to concretely identify
any of the operations required for "planet" or "spin
angular momentum" or "electron gyromagnetic moment", he is
still in the same pickle.

It does not say "definable in principle".  It says
definable in terms of *identifiable* operations.

So identify them!  Don't just wave your hands and claim
that in principle they could be identified.

Just how do you rotate an elementary particle by two pi? 
It can be done, but only through concepts, not through
operations.  The concepts define the operations, the
operations do not define the concepts.

This definition is totally consistent with my reading of
people who called themselves operationalists.  They seemed
to be ignorant philosophers - ignorant of philosophy and
even more ignorant of science, hostile to science and
hostile to the scientific method.  They appeared to be
arguing for the relativity and subjectivity of scientific
knowledge.

They objected to the scientific method of deducing facts
from experience, and claimed it to be invalid.  They
claimed that you ought to measure the properties of things
directly, rather than deduce them from observation, that to
deduce things from observation is unscientific, that the
way that science is commonly done is unscientific.

In making this claim they seemed to me to be seeking to
reverse the meaning of "scientific", so that only their
particular brand of philosophers could be properly called
scientific, and all other philosophers,  and all genuine
scientists were defined to be unscientific.


We use concepts to make predictions about the outcome of
experiments, but correct concepts cannot always be
expressed in terms of experimental outcomes.  They lead to
predictions of experimental outcomes, but they are not
themselves experimental outcomes.

An operational definition of "planet" would be full of
cumbersome detail concerning telescopes and photographs
that would be irrelevant to the concept of "planet".

